## E1. The verdicts

Each row is one question from Part D4, scored with the rules written down before running.
Negative energy means **less** energy.

{{ t.verdict_table }}

In plain words:

- **The rule makes both algorithms use less energy in every one of the 9 cases.** For
  LR-MMT the saving is small ({{ v.S1.total }} in total) and passes every test. For the
  memory-aware version it is large ({{ v.S2.total }} in total, {{ v.S2.large }} on the largest workload).
- **Why S2 still says "no":** {{ v.s2_fail_text }} The limits were fixed before running, so
  it counts as a "no", however close. E6 explains what crowding means here.
- **First Fit is still hard to beat.** LR-MMT + rule beats it in {{ v.S3.wins }} of 9 cases
  ({{ v.S3.total }} in total). Memory-aware + rule beats it in {{ v.S4.wins }} of 9 cases, every small and medium
  one, but not on the largest workload ({{ v.S4.large }}), so its total is {{ v.S4.total }}.
- **The team's original question (S5) turns into a narrow "yes":** with the rule, the
  memory-aware version beats LR-MMT in {{ v.S5.wins }} of 9 cases, but by only {{ v.S5.total }} in total, and it is
  still {{ v.S5.large }} on the largest workload.
- **With the rule on both (S6)**, caring about memory helps on small and medium workloads
  but not on the largest ({{ v.S6.large }}), so the total is {{ v.S6.total }}.

