## E4. Why: fewer servers on

On the largest workload, the memory-aware version kept **{{ v.servers.lr_mmt_memory }}** servers on, on
average. With the rule it keeps **{{ v.servers.lr_mmt_memory_same_job }}**, close to LR-MMT's {{ v.servers.lr_mmt }}.

<figure>
<img src="charts/servers_over_time.svg" alt="Servers switched on over the 12 hours">
<figcaption>Figure 4. The large workload on the HPE server model, in 30-minute averages. Top: servers switched on. Bottom: CPU load of the servers that are running work.</figcaption>
</figure>

The exact energy split from D5 (`RUSHI_Task/accounting.py`) says where the saving comes
from. Every server-second is charged in one state, so the parts add up exactly. Values
are the change in kWh per large (4,000-group) run when the rule is added, averaged over the
3 server models; negative = saves energy:

{{ t.split_table }}

For the memory-aware version the rule saves {{ v.split.mem_net }} kWh per large run, and
{{ v.split.mem_running_share }} of that is servers no longer kept on while running work. Wake-ups go up
({{ v.split.mem_wakes }}): servers that empty in one go fall asleep, so more of them have to be woken
later. That costs {{ v.split.mem_wake_cost }} kWh, far less than it saves. For LR-MMT the same pattern
is much smaller ({{ v.split.lr_net }} kWh per run).

<figure>
