</figure>

## E5. Did servers hold fewer jobs?

The rule did what it was designed to do: busy servers ran fewer different jobs at once.
Mean number of different jobs on a server that is running work (mean of the 3 server
models):

{{ t.mix_table }}

The change is biggest for the memory-aware version on the large workload
({{ v.mix.mem_before }} → {{ v.mix.mem_after }} jobs per busy server; servers running a single job went from
{{ v.mix.mem_single_before }} to {{ v.mix.mem_single_after }}), exactly where its energy fell most. For plain LR-MMT the change is
small ({{ v.mix.lr_before }} → {{ v.mix.lr_after }}), because LR-MMT already kept most of a job's tasks together,
and so is its saving.

## E6. Waiting and crowding

Saving energy only counts if the work is not noticeably slower. Large workload, mean of
the 3 server models:

{{ t.quality_table }}

- **Waiting barely changes:** the mean wait moves by {{ v.wait_change_lr }} s for LR-MMT and
  {{ v.wait_change_mem }} s for the memory-aware version, out of about {{ v.wait.lr_mmt }} s. On the large workload, bursts
  fill all 64 servers, so every algorithm makes some tasks wait.
- **Crowding (pressure) rises a little,** because fuller servers spend more time above
  90%. Memory-aware + rule ({{ v.pressure.lr_mmt_memory_same_job }}) is still **less** crowded than LR-MMT
  ({{ v.pressure.lr_mmt }}) and First Fit ({{ v.pressure.first_fit }}). The original memory-aware version was less crowded
  ({{ v.pressure.lr_mmt_memory }}) only because it kept about {{ v.extra_servers }} more servers on. That is the comparison
  (S2) with one case just over the limit.
{% if v.migrations_fall %}- **Migrations fall:** {{ v.migrations_change.lr_mmt }} for LR-MMT and {{ v.migrations_change.lr_mmt_memory }} for the memory-aware version.
{% endif %}
Remember that pressure is a warning sign, not real slowness: in this simulator a crowded
server does not slow down, because requests can never exceed capacity.

